WN.lb(38,{"mt_Uzlw605oVe":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A triangle drawn on a tangent has a rise of {=a*b} m and a run of {a} s. What is the speed, in m/s?","answer":"b","hint":"Gradient = rise ÷ run. Use the triangle on the tangent, not the curve.","explain":"{=a*b} ÷ {a} = {b}, so the instantaneous speed is {b} m/s.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"After {a} s a sprinter has run {=a*b} m. What is her average speed since the start, in m/s?","answer":"b","hint":"Average speed = total distance ÷ total time. You do not need a tangent for this one.","explain":"{=a*b} m ÷ {a} s = {b} m/s. That is the average; her speed at that moment can be different.","params":{"a":[2,8],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A tangent passes through (0 s, {a} m) and ({b} s, {=a+b*4} m). What is the speed at the point it touches, in m/s?","answer":"4","hint":"Rise = {=a+b*4} − {a}. Run = {b} − 0. Then work out rise ÷ run.","explain":"Rise = {=b*4} m and run = {b} s, so the speed is {=b*4} ÷ {b} = 4 m/s.","params":{"a":[0,10],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Zoe finds the speed at 3 s on a curved graph by working out (distance at 3 s) ÷ 3. What has she found?","choices":["The average speed from 0 to 3 s","The speed at exactly 3 s","The acceleration at 3 s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Distance ÷ time from the start is the gradient of which line: the tangent, or a line from the origin?","explain":"Distance ÷ time from the start is the gradient of the line from the origin, so it is the average speed from 0 to 3 s. The tangent gives the speed at 3 s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Two students draw different-sized triangles on the same tangent. What do they find?","choices":["The same gradient","The bigger triangle gives a bigger speed","The smaller triangle is more accurate"],"answer":0,"hint":"A straight line has the same steepness everywhere. Does the size of the triangle change rise ÷ run?","explain":"Rise and run grow together, so rise ÷ run stays the same: the same gradient. A big triangle is easier to read accurately."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A car brakes, so its distance–time curve gets flatter. Its speed at 2 s compared with its average speed from 0 to 2 s is…","choices":["Smaller","Bigger","The same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The car was going faster earlier. Is it faster or slower now than its average so far?","explain":"It was faster at the start and has been slowing, so its speed at 2 s is smaller than its average over the first 2 s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Max lays a ruler at P and it cuts through the curve, with the curve above it on one side and below it on the other. What should he do?","choices":["Turn the ruler until it only touches the curve at P","Use it: any line through P will do","Join P to the origin instead"],"answer":0,"hint":"Near P a tangent has the curve on the same side of it on both sides.","explain":"A line that cuts through the curve at P has the wrong gradient. He should turn the ruler until it only touches the curve at P; that tangent gives the speed."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A runner's curve is at 25 m at t = 5 s. Her tangent there rises 20 m over a run of 2 s. What is her speed at 5 s?","choices":["10 m/s","5 m/s","20 m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use only the tangent: rise ÷ run. The 25 m and 5 s give something else.","explain":"20 m ÷ 2 s = 10 m/s at 5 s. 25 ÷ 5 = 5 m/s is the average since the start, and 20 is the rise without dividing by the run."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A tangent to a distance–time curve rises {=a*b} m over a run of {b} s. What is the speed at that point, in m/s?","answer":"a","hint":"Speed is the gradient of the tangent: rise ÷ run. Divide {=a*b} by {b}.","explain":"{=a*b} m ÷ {b} s = {a} m/s, so the speed at that point is {a} m/s.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"At t = 4 s a curved distance–time graph is at 16 m and is getting steeper. What can you say about the speed at 4 s?","choices":["It is more than 4 m/s","It is exactly 4 m/s","It is 16 m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"16 ÷ 4 is the average since the start. If the graph is getting steeper, is the object faster or slower now than on average?","explain":"16 ÷ 4 = 4 m/s is the average since the start. The object is speeding up, so at 4 s it is going faster than 4 m/s. 16 is a distance, not a speed."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A tangent goes through (1 s, {a} m) and (4 s, {=a+3*b} m). What is the gradient of the tangent, in m/s?","answer":"b","hint":"Rise = {=a+3*b} − {a}. Run = 4 − 1 = 3 s. Then divide.","explain":"Rise = {=3*b} m, run = 3 s, so the gradient is {=3*b} ÷ 3 = {b} m/s.","params":{"a":[1,8],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which line is a tangent to a curve at a point P?","choices":["A straight line that touches the curve at P without cutting through it there","A line from the origin to P","A line joining P to the next point on the curve"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tangent has the same direction as the curve at P. Near P, the curve stays on one side of it.","explain":"A tangent just touches the curve at P. A line from the origin gives the average speed since the start, and a line to another point is a chord."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Part of a distance–time curve becomes flat. What is the speed there?","choices":["Zero: the object has stopped","Its highest value","The same as the distance"],"answer":0,"hint":"A flat tangent has a rise of 0. What is 0 ÷ run?","explain":"A flat tangent has gradient 0, so the speed is 0 m/s. The distance stays the same because the object has stopped."}],"explain":{"kid":["A distance–time graph shows how far something has gone. How steep it is shows how fast it is going. A steep part means fast. A flat part means stopped.","If the graph curves, the speed keeps changing. To find the speed at one moment, lay a ruler on the curve at that point. Turn it until it just touches the curve and does not cut through it. That line is called a tangent.","Now draw a big right-angled triangle on the tangent. Read the rise in metres and the run in seconds. Speed = rise ÷ run. If the rise is 12 m and the run is 2 s, the speed is 6 m/s.","Do not just divide the distance by the time. A runner who has gone 9 m in 3 s has an average of 3 m/s. If she is speeding up, she is going faster than that at 3 s."],"adult":["The gradient of a distance–time graph is speed, because it is distance gained per unit of time. On a straight section the gradient is constant. On a curve the gradient changes, and the instantaneous speed at time t is the gradient of the tangent to the curve at t.","To draw a tangent by hand, place a ruler at the point and turn it until it touches the curve without crossing it there. Near the point the curve should be on the same side of the ruler on both sides. Then draw a large right-angled triangle on the tangent and work out rise ÷ run from the axis scales, with units. A larger triangle gives the same gradient, but it is easier to read accurately.","Average speed over an interval is total distance ÷ total time, the gradient of the chord between two points. It is a different quantity. For an object speeding up, the instantaneous speed at the end of the interval is greater than the average over it. For an object slowing down, it is smaller. A flat tangent means a speed of zero.","Common mistakes are dividing the distance at the point by the time, giving the average since the start; reading the height of the graph as the speed; using a chord across a second instead of a tangent; and forgetting to divide the rise by the run. Be careful too that hand-drawn tangents give an estimate, so answers within a small range are accepted."]},"familyTip":"Sketch a curved distance–time graph for a car pulling away from traffic lights. Each pick a moment, lay a pencil on the curve as a tangent, and estimate the speed with a rise ÷ run triangle. Compare your answers: how close did two hand-drawn tangents get?","keyIdea":"On a distance–time graph the gradient is the speed. When the graph curves, the speed at one instant is the gradient of the tangent at that point: a straight line that just touches the curve there. Find it as rise ÷ run from a triangle on the tangent. Distance ÷ time from the start gives the average speed instead.","worked":{"title":"A runner's graph passes through (3 s, 9 m). A tangent drawn there passes through (1 s, −3 m) and (5 s, 21 m). How fast is she going at 3 s?","steps":["Use two points far apart on the tangent, not on the curve: (1, −3) and (5, 21).","Rise = 21 − (−3) = 24 m. Run = 5 − 1 = 4 s.","Speed = rise ÷ run = 24 ÷ 4 = 6 m/s.","Compare: her average speed since the start is 9 ÷ 3 = 3 m/s. She is speeding up, so 6 m/s at 3 s makes sense."]},"visual":{"type":"plot","caption":"Drag the point along a curved distance–time graph. The tangent line shows the gradient there: the speed at that instant.","opts":{"mode":"tangent","f":"x*x","a":3,"xr":[0,6],"yr":[0,36],"step":0.5}},"lab":{"type":"flagtan","caption":"Flagship lab: turn a ruler on a curved distance–time graph until it is a tangent, then read the speed as rise ÷ run.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_ckSChWLbN7":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"You kick a ball. Is that a push or a pull?","choices":["A push","A pull","Not a push or a pull"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the ball go away from you or come to you?","explain":"Your foot sends the ball away, so a kick is a push."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which push makes a toy car go the furthest?","choices":["A strong push","A gentle push","A tiny tap"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think big push, little push.","explain":"A strong push makes the car go fastest, so it rolls the furthest."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The goal is on your left. Where do you push the ball from?","choices":["From its right side","From its left side","From on top"],"answer":0,"hint":"A ball rolls away from the push.","explain":"Push from the right and the ball rolls left, away from your hand, into the goal."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A door has a sign that says PUSH. How do you open it?","choices":["Push it away from you","Pull it towards you","Lift it up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read the sign. It tells you which way to move the door.","explain":"A PUSH door opens away from you, so you push it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The swing only goes up a little. What will make it go higher?","choices":["A bigger push","A smaller push","No push"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about big and gentle pushes.","explain":"A bigger push makes the swing go higher. A gentle one only moves it a little."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You tug a rope towards you. Is that a push or a pull?","choices":["A pull","A push","Not a push or a pull"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way does the rope move?","explain":"The rope comes towards you, so tugging is a pull."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A gentle push rolls a ball {n} steps. A strong push rolls it twice as far. How many steps is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Twice as far means {n} and {n} again.","explain":"{n} and {n} more is {=2*n} steps.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"You open {n} drawers. Each one needs 1 pull to open and 1 push to shut. How many pushes and pulls is that?","answer":"2*n","hint":"Count 2 for each drawer: a pull and a push.","explain":"Each drawer needs 2, so {n} drawers need {=2*n} pushes and pulls.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"You pull a wagon. Which way does it go?","choices":["Towards you","Away from you","It does not move"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pull brings things to you.","explain":"A pull brings the wagon towards you. A push would send it away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A gentle push rolls the car {n} steps. A strong push rolls it 2 steps more. How far does the strong push go?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more steps.","explain":"{n} and 2 more is {=n+2} steps. The strong push goes further.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you open a drawer?","choices":["Pull the handle","Push it in","Shout at it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the drawer come towards you or go away?","explain":"The drawer comes out towards you, so you pull it. You push it to shut it."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You give the swing {n} big pushes and then 1 more. How many pushes is that?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on one more from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1} pushes.","params":{"n":[2,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Kick a ball and it goes away from you. That is a push. Tug a rope and it comes to you. That is a pull.","Give a toy car a little push. It rolls a little way. Give it a big push. Off it goes, a long way! A big push makes it go further.","Push a ball from this side, and it rolls the other way. It goes away from your hand. So stand on the side away from the goal.","To open a drawer, pull the handle to you. To shut it, push it in. A swing goes up high when you give it big pushes."],"adult":["This is early physics. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to explore how things work and to talk about the forces they feel, such as a push or a pull. In Year 3 children learn that a force is a push or a pull, and later that forces can change the speed, direction and shape of objects. The aim now is the words push, pull, harder, gentle, faster, further and direction, linked to things a child has done.","The science under the play: a push acts away from the person and a pull acts towards them. A larger force on the same toy gives it more speed, so it rolls further before friction stops it. A ball moves off in the direction of the push, so you push from the side opposite where you want it to go. A swing goes higher when each push adds more energy.","Common mistakes: thinking a push brings things towards you; calling every action with the hands a push; expecting a gentle push to send a car as far as a strong one; pushing a ball from the goal side and expecting it to go in; pushing a drawer to open it. Ask them to say which way the thing moved: away from me, or towards me?","In the lab your child sorts actions such as kicking a ball and opening a drawer into push or pull, drags a hand back to give a car a gentle, middle or strong push and parks it in a spot, pushes a ball from the side that sends it into the goal, opens and shuts a drawer and a door, and pushes a swing up to a star."]},"familyTip":"Play with a ball or a toy car on the floor. Ask: is that a push or a pull? Try a little push and a big push and mark where it stops with a sock. Which went further?","keyIdea":"A push moves a thing away from you and a pull brings it towards you. Pushes and pulls are forces: they make things start to move, go faster, change direction or stop. A bigger push makes a toy car go further and a swing go higher; a gentle push only moves it a little. A thing moves away from the side you push it from. You pull a drawer to open it and push it to shut it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Push or pull? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["➡️ Push: away from me","⬅️ Pull: towards me"],"items":[["kick a ball",0],["open a drawer",1],["pull a sock off",1],["push a door that says PUSH",0],["pull a wagon by its handle",1],["press a doorbell",0],["tug a rope",1],["push a pram",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshove","caption":"Flagship lab: sort pushes and pulls, push a car, a ball and a swing, and open a drawer and a door.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_E4A6SWoxYo":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which ramp makes a toy car go fastest?","choices":["The steepest one","The flattest one","They are all the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ramp had the most books under it in the race?","explain":"The steepest ramp makes the car go fastest, so it also goes furthest on the floor."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A tin can stands on its flat end at the top of a slope. What happens?","choices":["It stays put","It rolls down","It rolls up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it touching the slope with its round side or its flat end?","explain":"On its flat end it stays put. Lay it on its curved side and it rolls."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which of these will roll down a slope?","choices":["An orange","A book","A sponge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one that is round, with no flat sides.","explain":"An orange is round, so it rolls. A book and a sponge have flat sides, so they stay."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A big heavy brick and a small light ball sit on a slope. Which one rolls?","choices":["The light ball","The heavy brick","Neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does weight decide, or does shape decide?","explain":"Shape decides. The ball is round, so it rolls, even though it is light. The brick stays."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The car stopped before the teddy. How can you make it reach the teddy?","choices":["Add a book to make it steeper","Take a book away","Use a bigger teddy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Steeper sends the car further. Gentler stops it sooner.","explain":"Add a book. A steeper slope makes the car go faster, so it rolls further to the teddy."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A ramp sits on {n} books. You take 1 book away. How many books are left?","answer":"n-1","hint":"Start at {n} and count back 1.","explain":"{n} books take away 1 leaves {=n-1}. The slope is gentler, so the car stops sooner.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A car rolls {n} big steps from a low ramp. From a steeper ramp it goes 2 steps further. How far now?","answer":"n+2","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 2 more steps.","explain":"{n} steps and 2 more make {=n+2} steps. The steeper ramp sent the car further.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You want a fair test of slopes. What should you change?","choices":["Only the number of books","The car and the books","Everything at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"If you change the car too, can you tell which change made the difference?","explain":"Change only the books and keep the same car. Then you know the slope made the difference."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Sam lets go of a ball and a brick at the top of a slope. Which one rolls down?","choices":["The ball","The brick","Both of them"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about shape. Which one is round all the way round?","explain":"The ball is round, so it rolls. The brick has flat sides, so it stays put."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ramp sits on {n} books. Mia adds 1 more book to make it steeper. How many books now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Start at {n} and count on 1 more book.","explain":"{n} books and 1 more make {=n+1} books. The slope is steeper, so the car will go further.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Ana wants her car to go further. What should she do to the ramp?","choices":["Make it steeper","Make it less steep","Paint it red"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the race. Which ramp sent its car further?","explain":"A steeper slope makes the car go faster, so it rolls further across the floor."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo tests {n} balls and {k} bricks on a slope. Only the round ones roll. How many things roll?","answer":"n","hint":"Bricks have flat sides. Count only the balls.","explain":"Only the {n} balls roll, because they are round. The {k} bricks stay put, so {=n} things roll.","params":{"n":[1,5],"k":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Put a board on some books. Now it is a slope, like a slide.","Let go of a ball at the top. Down it rolls! A car with wheels rolls too. A brick has flat sides. It stays put.","A tin can is round. But stand it on its flat end and it does not roll. Lay it on its curved side and away it goes.","Add more books. The slope is steeper now. The car goes faster and rolls further on the floor."],"adult":["In England, Development Matters (2023) asks 3 and 4 year olds to explore how things work and to explore and talk about forces they can feel, and Reception children to talk about the differences between materials and the changes they notice. In the US, kindergarten standards K-PS2-1 and K-PS2-2 ask children to compare how different pushes change an object's motion and to test a design that changes an object's speed or direction. A ramp is the classic first investigation for both.","The science, in words to use with your child: gravity pulls things down the slope. Round things (a ball, a wheel, a can on its side) touch the slope at one line or point and turn over and over, so they roll. Things with flat faces (a brick, a book, a sponge) grip the board and stay, or slide if the slope is very steep. A steeper slope means the car starts higher, so it is going faster at the bottom and rolls further before the floor slows it down.","Common mistakes to listen for: thinking heavy things roll and light things do not (shape decides it, not weight); thinking anything round always rolls (a can standing on its flat end stays put); and thinking a gentler slope is faster because it is longer. Be careful to keep it a fair test: use the same car, let go from the very top without a push, and change only the number of books.","In the lab your child stacks books and lets the car go from different heights, with a flag showing where each run stops; tests five toys to see which roll; tips a tin can, a kitchen roll and a crayon onto their sides; races a steep ramp against a gentle one; and picks the slope that stops the car by a teddy. Every drag has a big button twin."]},"familyTip":"Lean a tray or a big book on a cushion and roll a toy car down it. Mark where it stops with a sock. Add a second cushion and try again. Ask: did it go further? Then try a ball, a block and a cup lying on its side.","keyIdea":"Prop a board on books and you have a ramp, a slope. Let go of a ball or a toy car at the top and the Earth pulls it down. Round things roll. Things with flat sides stay put or slide. Add more books and the slope gets steeper, so the car goes faster and further across the floor. A fair test changes only one thing: same car, more books.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Rolls or stays? Drag each card to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Rolls","Stays"],"items":[["ball",0],["brick",1],["orange",0],["book",1],["tin can on its side",0],["tin can on its flat end",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagramp","caption":"Flagship lab: stack books to make the slope steeper, test which toys roll, tip a can onto its side, race two ramps, stop by a teddy.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FiH6RZTODa":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A bag is blown up and shut tight. What is inside?","choices":["Air","Nothing","Water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Squeeze it. Does it push back?","explain":"It is full of air. That is why it pushes back when you squeeze."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which one can a puff of air move?","choices":["A feather","A big rock","A house"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it light, or is it very heavy to lift?","explain":"A feather is light, so a puff of air moves it. A rock and a house are too heavy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"You push a cup upside down into water. Is the tissue inside wet?","choices":["No, air keeps it dry","Yes, it gets wet","It floats out"],"answer":0,"hint":"What is in the cup before the water?","explain":"Air fills the cup, so the water cannot get in. The tissue stays dry."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How can you feel air?","choices":["Wave a hand by your face","Look very hard","Hold your breath"],"answer":0,"hint":"Air has no colour. Can you feel it move?","explain":"Waving moves the air, and you feel it on your face."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"The wind moves {n} leaves. {k} leaves stay still under a rock. How many leaves in all?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Count the moving leaves, then count on the still ones.","explain":"{n} leaves and {k} more make {=n+k} leaves.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You tip the cup in the water. What comes up?","choices":["Bubbles of air","Nothing","Sand"],"answer":0,"hint":"What was inside the cup before you tipped it?","explain":"The air comes out in bubbles, and the water goes in."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You pop {p} of your {b} bubbles. How many bubbles are left?","answer":"b-p","hint":"Start at {b} and take away the {p} you popped.","explain":"{b} take away {p} leaves {=b-p} bubbles.","params":{"b":[4,6],"p":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is air still there when it is not windy?","choices":["Yes, air is all around us","No, it goes away","Only in a bag"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wave your hand. Can you still feel air?","explain":"Yes. Air is all around us all the time. Wind is just air on the move."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What pushes the kite up in the sky?","choices":["The wind","The string","The clouds"],"answer":0,"hint":"What moves outside on a windy day?","explain":"The wind, moving air, pushes the kite. The string holds it."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Can you see air?","choices":["No, but we can feel it","Yes, it is blue","No, so it is not there"],"answer":0,"hint":"Wave a hand by your face. What do you feel?","explain":"We cannot see air, but we can feel it move. It is all around us."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You wave the fan {n} times. Each wave pushes the feather 1 step. How many steps does it go?","answer":"n","hint":"Count 1 step for each wave.","explain":"{n} waves push the feather {n} steps. Each wave moves the air.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You blow {a} bubbles. Then you blow {b} more. How many bubbles of air?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at {a} and count on {b} more.","explain":"{a} and {b} more make {=a+b} bubbles. Each one has air inside.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What makes the wind?","choices":["Moving air","The trees waving","The clouds"],"answer":0,"hint":"What pushes the trees and makes them wave?","explain":"Wind is air on the move. It pushes the trees, so they wave."}],"explain":{"kid":["Air is all around you. You cannot see it. But you can feel it. Wave your hand by your face. That is air!","Moving air can push. Wave a fan and a feather flies. The wind is air on the move. It pushes leaves and kites.","A bag can look empty. But it is full of air. Squeeze it shut and it pushes back. A bubble has air in it too.","Push a cup down in water, upside down. The air in the cup keeps the water out. Tip it, and the air comes out in bubbles."],"adult":["In England this sits in the EYFS area Understanding the World. Development Matters (2021) asks 3 and 4 year olds to explore and talk about different forces they can feel, and the early learning goal The Natural World asks children to explore the world around them and talk about what they notice. In the US, the Head Start Early Learning Outcomes Framework puts it under Scientific Reasoning: observe and describe objects and events.","Air is a real thing even when it is still. It is a mix of gases, mostly nitrogen and oxygen. It has no colour, but it fills spaces and pushes on things. Wind is air moving from one place to another. A fan or a breath makes air move too. Light things move easily; heavy things like a rock do not move in a gentle wind.","Common mistakes to watch for: young children often say a bag or cup is empty when it is full of air; many think air is only there when it moves; and many say the trees make the wind by waving, which Piaget recorded in the 1920s and teachers still hear. Name air out loud, let them feel it push, and ask what moved the leaf.","In the lab your child waves a fan to push a feather into a box, catches air in a bag and squeezes it, blows and pops bubbles, finds what the wind moves outside, and pushes an upside-down cup into water so the paper inside stays dry. Every step works with big buttons or by tapping the picture. Read the Challenge aloud together."]},"familyTip":"Tuck a dry tissue into the bottom of a cup. Turn it upside down and push it straight down into a bowl of water. Lift it out and feel the tissue: dry. Then try it tipped. On a windy day, look for 3 things the wind moves.","keyIdea":"Air is all around us, even though we cannot see it. We can feel it when it moves, and moving air can push light things: a fan pushes a feather, and the wind pushes leaves, kites and washing. A bag that looks empty is full of air, and a bubble has air inside. Air takes up space: push an upside-down cup into water and the air inside keeps the paper dry. Wind is air on the move; the trees do not make it.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Can a puff of air move it? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A puff of air moves it","Too heavy for a puff"],"items":[["Feather",0],["Big rock",1],["Bubble",0],["Leaf",0],["Table",1],["Paper boat on water",0],["Car",1],["Tissue",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagair","caption":"Flagship lab: wave a fan to push a feather, catch air in a bag, pop bubbles, find what the wind moves and keep paper dry in a cup under water.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_pKLzsX2Lsa":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A magnet pulls {a} pins and {b} bolts. How many things did it pull?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Pins and bolts are both steel. Add them.","explain":"Pins and bolts are steel, so all of them are pulled: {a} and {b} more make {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A box has {a} paper clips and 3 crayons. How many things will a magnet pull out?","answer":"a","hint":"Crayons are wax. Which things are steel?","explain":"Only the {a} paper clips are steel, so the magnet pulls out {a}. Crayons are not pulled.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which one will a magnet NOT pull?","choices":["Paper","A paper clip","A bolt"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two are steel. Which one is not?","explain":"Paper is not pulled. The paper clip and bolt are steel, so the magnet pulls them."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Does a magnet pull all metal things?","choices":["No, only some","Yes, all metal","It pulls no metal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the gold ring and the trumpet. Were they pulled?","explain":"A magnet pulls iron and steel, but not every metal. Gold and brass are not pulled."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Paper is between a magnet and a clip. What happens?","choices":["The magnet still pulls the clip","Nothing, paper stops it","The paper sticks to the magnet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Remember the clip that went home.","explain":"A magnet can pull through thin paper, so the clip still moves. The paper itself is not pulled."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"When does a magnet pull a paper clip best?","choices":["When it is close","When it is far away","When it is red"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happened when you moved the magnet closer?","explain":"The pull is strongest close up. Far away it may not pull at all. Colour does not matter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A gold ring is metal. Will a magnet pull it?","choices":["No","Yes, it is metal","Yes, if it is shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do magnets pull every metal?","explain":"Gold is a metal, but magnets do not pull gold. They pull iron and steel."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"The magnet pulls {a} paper clips and {b} pins. How many things did it pull?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Count the clips. Then count on the pins.","explain":"Clips and pins are both steel, so the magnet pulls all of them: {a} and {b} more make {=a+b}.","params":{"a":[1,4],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {a} fish. Only 2 have a paper clip. How many fish will the magnet not catch?","answer":"a-2","hint":"Fish with no clip are not pulled. Take away the 2 with clips.","explain":"The magnet only catches the 2 fish with clips. {a} take away 2 is {=a-2} fish it will not catch.","params":{"a":[3,7]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You test {a} socks and {b} pins with a magnet. How many things does it pull?","answer":"b","hint":"Does a magnet pull cloth? Does it pull steel pins?","explain":"Socks are cloth, so none of them are pulled. The {b} pins are steel, so the magnet pulls {b} things.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which one will a magnet pull?","choices":["A safety pin","A sock","A wooden block"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is made of steel?","explain":"A safety pin is steel, so the magnet pulls it. A sock is cloth and a block is wood: no pull."}],"explain":{"kid":["A magnet can pull some things to it. Hold it near a paper clip. Click! The clip jumps to the magnet.","A magnet does not pull a sock. It does not pull paper or wood. They stay still.","A magnet pulls things made of iron or steel. A pin is steel. A bolt is steel. Gold and brass are metal too, but a magnet does not pull them.","A magnet pulls best when it is close. Far away, it may not pull at all. It can even pull through thin paper."],"adult":["Early years science and the English national curriculum both start magnets with hands-on play. Year 3 then asks pupils to 'observe how magnets attract or repel each other and attract some materials and not others' and to 'compare and group together a variety of everyday materials on the basis of whether they are attracted to a magnet, and identify some magnetic materials'. In the US, kindergarten pushes and pulls lead to 3-PS2-3 on magnetic forces.","A magnet attracts magnetic materials: iron, steel, nickel and cobalt. Everyday magnetic things are mostly iron or steel, such as paper clips, safety pins, bolts and food tins. Paper, cloth, wood and plastic are not attracted. Neither are most other metals, including gold, brass, copper and aluminium. The pull is a non-contact force: it acts across a gap and through thin paper, and it gets weaker quickly with distance.","Common mistakes: thinking a magnet pulls every metal, or anything shiny; thinking it pulls anything at all, such as paper or a sock; believing paper or a table blocks the pull; and thinking a bigger or redder magnet is always stronger. Be careful with coins and keys, which may or may not be steel. Test them rather than guess.","In the lab your child tries a magnet on six things, fishes for the three fish that carry a paper clip, drags a magnet under paper to pull a clip home, moves a magnet closer until the clip jumps, and predicts whether a tin can, gold ring, scissors and a trumpet will be pulled."]},"familyTip":"Go on a magnet hunt round the kitchen with a fridge magnet. Before each test, ask 'Will it pull?' Then try it. Fridge doors and tins usually pull; wooden spoons and plastic boxes do not.","keyIdea":"A magnet pulls, or attracts, some things to it. It pulls things made of iron or steel, like paper clips, pins and bolts. It does not pull paper, cloth, wood or plastic, and it does not pull every metal: gold and brass are not pulled. A magnet pulls best close up, and it can pull through thin paper.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Will the magnet pull it? Drag each thing into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Pulls","No pull"],"items":[["Paper clip",0],["Sock",1],["Safety pin",0],["Paper",1],["Steel bolt",0],["Wooden spoon",1],["Food tin",0],["Gold ring",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmag","caption":"Flagship lab: try a magnet on six things, go magnet fishing, pull a clip through paper, move the magnet closer, and test four metal things.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_k7GOtslF-x":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does the length of a force arrow show?","choices":["how big the force is","how far the object moves","how heavy the arrow is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about drawing a 20 N force and a 40 N force on the same scale.","explain":"A longer arrow means a bigger force. The arrow's direction shows which way the force acts."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"In a tug of war the left team pulls 60 N and the right team pulls 40 N. What is the resultant force?","choices":["20 N to the left","100 N to the left","20 N to the right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opposite ways: take the smaller from the bigger. Which way is the bigger pull?","explain":"60 − 40 = 20 N, pointing left, the way of the bigger pull. The rope moves to the left."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"Two children push a cart the same way, {a} N and {b} N. What is the resultant force, in N?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Both pushes act the same way, so add them.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} N. Forces that act the same way add together.","params":{"a":[10,40],"b":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The forces on a rolling ball become balanced. What happens to the ball?","choices":["it keeps rolling at the same speed","it stops straight away","it rolls faster and faster"],"answer":0,"hint":"Balanced forces cause no change in motion.","explain":"Balanced forces mean no change, so the ball keeps the same speed and direction. It only slows down if the forces become unbalanced, for example from more friction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A parachutist falls at a steady speed. Weight is 700 N down. What is the air resistance?","choices":["700 N up","0 N","1400 N up"],"answer":0,"hint":"A steady speed means the forces are balanced.","explain":"At a steady speed the forces balance, so air resistance is 700 N up, equal and opposite to the 700 N weight."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sledge is pulled {a} N forward. Friction is {b} N backward. What is the resultant force, in N?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Opposite ways, so subtract the smaller force from the bigger one.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} N forward. The forces are unbalanced, so the sledge speeds up.","params":{"a":[50,80],"b":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"On a diagram 1 square = 10 N. Arrow A is 2 squares long and arrow B is 6 squares long. Which is true?","choices":["B shows a force 3 times as big as A","A shows the bigger force","they show the same force"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turn each length into newtons first.","explain":"A is 2 × 10 = 20 N and B is 6 × 10 = 60 N, so B is 3 times as big. Longer arrow, bigger force."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A football is still on the grass. Then it is kicked. Why does it start to move?","choices":["the kick makes the forces unbalanced","the forces on it stay balanced","gravity stops pulling on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"What has to happen to the forces for something still to start moving?","explain":"The kick adds a big force one way, so the forces are unbalanced and the ball starts to move. Gravity still pulls on it all the time."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A cup sits still on a table. Its weight is 3 N down. Which force arrow is missing from the diagram?","choices":["3 N up from the table","3 N down from the table","no arrow, the table does not push"],"answer":0,"hint":"The cup is still, so the forces must balance. What does the table do to the cup?","explain":"The table pushes up on the cup with 3 N, equal and opposite to the 3 N weight. Balanced forces, so the cup stays still."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"On squared paper 1 square = 10 N. How many squares long is the arrow for a {=a*10} N force?","answer":"a","hint":"Divide the force by 10 N, the size of one square.","explain":"{=a*10} N ÷ 10 N = {a}, so the arrow is {a} squares long.","params":{"a":[2,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A box is pushed {a} N to the right. Friction pulls it {b} N to the left. What is the size of the resultant force, in N?","answer":"a-b","hint":"The forces act in opposite ways, so take the smaller from the bigger.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} N. It points to the right, the way of the bigger force, so the forces are unbalanced.","params":{"a":[40,90],"b":[10,30]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A boat moves at a steady speed. The engine pushes it forward with 500 N. How big is the water resistance?","choices":["500 N","0 N","more than 500 N"],"answer":0,"hint":"A steady speed means the speed is not changing. What does that tell you about the forces?","explain":"Steady speed means the forces are balanced, so the water resistance is also 500 N, backwards. Moving does not need an unbalanced force."}],"explain":{"kid":["A force is a push or a pull. We can draw it as an arrow. The arrow points the way the push or pull goes.","A long arrow is a big force. A short arrow is a small force. We count force in newtons, or N. On squared paper, 1 square can be 10 N.","Two forces that pull in opposite ways: take the small one from the big one. Two that push the same way: add them. The answer is the resultant force.","If the forces are equal and opposite, they are balanced. A still thing stays still. A moving thing keeps going at the same speed. Be careful: balanced does not mean stopped."],"adult":["England's Key Stage 3 science programme asks for 'force arrows in diagrams, adding forces in one dimension, balanced and unbalanced forces'. It treats forces as pushes or pulls between two objects. In Year 5 children meet gravity, air resistance, water resistance and friction. They measure forces in newtons (N) with a newton meter.","A good force diagram has one arrow for each force. Each arrow starts on the object and points the way the force acts. Its length is drawn to a scale, and its label gives the name and the size. To find the resultant force, add forces that act the same way. Take away forces that act the other way. The resultant points the way of the bigger total.","Common mistakes: many children think a moving thing needs a bigger forward force just to keep going. So they expect balanced forces to stop a bike. In fact balanced forces mean no change in motion, so the bike keeps a steady speed. This is Newton's first law. Others think a table cannot push, so they leave out the support force on a still book. Some add forces that act opposite ways. Some draw arrows with no scale, so a 40 N arrow ends up shorter than a 20 N one.","In the lab your child balances a book's 20 N weight with the table's push. Next they draw a 40 N push 4 squares long. Then they find the resultant of a tug of war, 30 N against 50 N, and add two pushes that act the same way. Last, they balance a moving bike's pedal push with air resistance and see that it keeps a steady speed."]},"familyTip":"Push a toy car across the floor and stop pushing: ask which force slows it down (friction). Then hold a heavy book flat on your hand and ask which way your hand is pushing and how you know. Sketch the arrows together on squared paper, one square for each 10 N, and label each one.","keyIdea":"A force diagram shows each force on an object as a labelled arrow. The arrow points the way the force acts, and its length shows the size of the force, drawn to scale (for example 1 square = 10 newtons). Forces acting opposite ways are subtracted and forces acting the same way are added; the answer is the resultant force, drawn as a single arrow. Equal and opposite forces are balanced, with a resultant of 0 N, so the object's motion does not change: a still object stays still and a moving one keeps a steady speed. Unbalanced forces make it speed up, slow down or change direction.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Are the forces balanced or unbalanced? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Balanced","Unbalanced"],"items":[["A book resting on a table",0],["A tug of war that is not moving",0],["A car at a steady speed on a straight road",0],["A ball hanging still on a string",0],["A bike speeding up downhill",1],["A football just kicked from still",1],["A sledge slowing down on grass",1],["An apple falling faster and faster",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfdiag","caption":"Flagship lab: drag arrow tips (1 square = 10 N) to balance a book, draw a push to scale, find two resultants and balance a moving bike.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_GBY8enpzO0":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Gravity is…","choices":["non-contact","only a contact push","not a force"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gravity pulls without needing a hand on you.","explain":"Gravity is a non-contact force acting at a distance."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A sled slowing on snow meets…","choices":["friction","only gravity upward","twist"],"answer":0,"hint":"Slowing while sliding names friction.","explain":"Friction is the contact force that opposes the slide."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Weight on Earth links to…","choices":["gravity","only air resistance","only stretch"],"answer":0,"hint":"Weight is the pull of gravity on a mass.","explain":"Weight is the gravitational force on an object."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Kick a ball away: that is a…","choices":["push","pull","non-contact only"],"answer":0,"hint":"The ball moves away from your foot.","explain":"A kick away from you is a push."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You name {n} non-contact examples. How many?","answer":"n","hint":"Gravity and magnets are typical non-contact examples.","explain":"Listing {n} non-contact examples means {=n} examples.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Squash clay {n} times. How many squash actions?","answer":"n","hint":"Each squash is one shape-changing force action.","explain":"{n} squashes are {=n} squash actions.","params":{"n":[2,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Air resistance…","choices":["slows things in air","is not a force","only stretches springs"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a parachute slowing as it falls.","explain":"Air resistance is a force that opposes motion through air."},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: build force vocabulary.","items":["feel push or pull","name contact or non-contact","name friction or gravity when it fits"],"hint":"Feel first, then sort by contact, then precise names.","explain":"Start from felt pushes/pulls, then contact type, then exact words."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Opening a drawer is usually a…","choices":["pull","push","twist only"],"answer":0,"hint":"The drawer comes toward you when it opens.","explain":"Pulling brings the drawer toward you; that is a pull force."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Making a spring longer is…","choices":["stretch","squash","gravity"],"answer":0,"hint":"Longer means the force named stretch.","explain":"Stretch is the force action that increases length."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You give {n} equal pushes. How many push forces is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Count each push as one force action.","explain":"{n} pushes means {=n} push actions in this count.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A sled needs {n} friction reminders to stop. How many?","answer":"n","hint":"Friction is the slowing contact force each time.","explain":"You name friction {n} times; that is {=n} friction reminders.","params":{"n":[1,5]}},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Order: name forces on a slide.","items":["push off at the top","gravity pulls you down","friction slows near the end"],"hint":"Start the motion, fall with gravity, then slow.","explain":"Push starts you; gravity acts down the slide; friction slows you."}],"explain":{"kid":["A push moves something away from you. A pull brings it toward you.","Stretch makes something longer. Squash makes it shorter. Twist turns it.","Some forces need a touch. Gravity and magnets can pull without touching.","Friction and air resistance are forces that slow things down."],"adult":["Force words: push, pull, twist, stretch, squash; touch forces vs forces at a distance (gravity, magnets); friction; air resistance. Link each word to something your child feels.","Common mistakes: calling every hand action a push; thinking gravity needs a touch; saying friction makes you go; swapping stretch and squash; saying air resistance is not a force.","In the lab your child pushes and pulls a car, stretches a spring, drops with gravity, slides with friction, and matches the force words."]},"familyTip":"On a walk name forces: 'push the swing', 'pull the door', 'friction on your shoes', 'gravity pulls you down the slide'.","keyIdea":"Forces have clear names: push and pull; stretch, squash and twist for shape; touch forces versus gravity and magnets; friction and air resistance that slow things down.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Match each scene to a force word. Drag, or tap a card then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Push / pull","Shape change","Non-contact","Slows motion"],"items":[["kick a ball away",0],["open a drawer toward you",0],["stretch a spring",1],["squash clay flatter",1],["gravity on a falling ball",2],["magnet pulls without touch",2],["sled slows on snow",3],["parachute slowed by air",3]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfvoc","caption":"Flagship lab: push/pull, stretch/squash/twist, gravity vs contact, friction/air, match words.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_jgNB2752b9":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Where is the pull of a bar magnet strongest?","choices":["At the two poles","In the middle","The same all along"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about where the paper clips stuck.","explain":"The pull is strongest at the poles, the two ends. Hardly any clips stick to the middle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"You snap {a} bar magnets in half. How many poles do all the pieces have?","answer":"4*a","hint":"Each half becomes a whole magnet with its own N and S.","explain":"Each magnet makes 2 pieces with 2 poles each, so 4 poles per magnet: {a} × 4 = {=4*a} poles.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{a} magnets in a row all point north when they hang free. How many N poles point north?","answer":"a","hint":"Each magnet has exactly one north pole.","explain":"Every free magnet turns one N pole to the north, so {a} N poles point north.","params":{"a":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ben says, 'N and N will stick together because they match.' Is he right?","choices":["No, like poles repel","Yes, matching poles attract","Only if the magnets are big"],"answer":0,"hint":"Try it with the lab: what did N meeting N do?","explain":"Ben has it the wrong way round. Like poles, such as N and N, push apart. Unlike poles attract."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A metal bar is pulled towards BOTH ends of your magnet. What is it?","choices":["Plain iron, not a magnet","Surely a magnet","A broken magnet"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would a magnet be pulled by both of your poles?","explain":"A magnet would push away one of your poles. Plain iron is pulled by both, so a pull alone proves nothing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"End A of a mystery magnet pushes away your N pole. What is end A?","choices":["A north pole","A south pole","Not a pole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which poles push each other away?","explain":"Only like poles repel. End A pushed your N away, so end A is a north pole too."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which test proves an object is a magnet?","choices":["It pushes away one pole of a magnet","A magnet pulls it","It is shiny"],"answer":0,"hint":"Iron is pulled too. What can only a magnet do?","explain":"A magnet pulls iron as well as other magnets, so only a push, repulsion, proves the object is a magnet."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"You flip one magnet round. The ends were pulling. What happens now?","choices":["They push apart","They still pull","They stop doing anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Flipping one magnet swaps which pole faces the other.","explain":"They were unlike poles. After the flip the facing poles are alike, so they repel."},{"type":"order","id":"m9","prompt":"Put the steps in order to find the N pole of an unlabelled magnet.","items":["Bring your magnet's N pole near one end","Watch for a push or a pull","If it pushes, that end is N"],"hint":"Test first, watch, then decide.","explain":"Bring your N pole close, watch what happens, and use the push: like poles repel, so a push means that end is N."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"An N pole faces an S pole. What happens?","choices":["They pull together","They push apart","Nothing happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"N and S are different poles. Are they like or unlike?","explain":"N and S are unlike poles, and unlike poles attract. The magnets pull together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A box holds {a} bar magnets. How many poles are there in total?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Every magnet has a north pole and a south pole.","explain":"Each magnet has 2 poles, so {a} magnets have {=2*a} poles.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"You test a bar magnet with clips. The two ends hold {a} clips each and the middle holds {b}. How many clips in all?","answer":"2*a+b","hint":"Two ends with the same number each, then add the middle.","explain":"The ends hold {a} + {a} = {=2*a} and the middle adds {b}, so {=2*a+b} clips. Most cling at the poles.","params":{"a":[4,9],"b":[0,1]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"An S pole faces another S pole. What happens?","choices":["They push apart","They pull together","Nothing happens"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both ends are S. Same poles behave the same way as N and N.","explain":"S and S are like poles, and like poles repel. They push apart just as N and N do."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A bar magnet hangs from a thread and stops turning. Where does its N pole point?","choices":["North","South","Any way at all"],"answer":0,"hint":"The poles were named after the way they point.","explain":"A freely hanging magnet lines up so its north-seeking pole points north. That is why it is called the north pole."}],"explain":{"kid":["A magnet has two ends called poles. One is the north pole, N. The other is the south pole, S.","Hang a magnet so it can turn. It stops with its N pole pointing north. That is how a compass works.","Put two magnets end to end. N and S pull together. We say unlike poles attract. N and N push apart. So do S and S. We say like poles repel. You can feel the push.","The pull is strongest at the poles. That is why paper clips cling to the ends, not the middle."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 9. England NC Year 3 forces and magnets: describe magnets as having two poles, and predict whether two magnets will attract or repel each other depending on which poles are facing. NGSS 3-PS2-3 covers the same magnetic interactions.","A freely suspended magnet lines up with Earth's magnetic field, so the end that points north is called the north-seeking pole, shortened to north pole. The field, and so the pull, is strongest at the poles.","Common mistakes: thinking like poles attract; thinking N and S are like plus and minus charges; expecting the strongest pull in the middle; thinking a broken magnet keeps one pole per piece, when each piece gets an N and an S; and treating attraction as proof of a magnet, when plain iron is attracted too. Only repulsion proves it.","In the lab your child swings a magnet to north, feels attraction and repulsion by dragging, fills a four-row results table, dips the ends and middle in clips, and uses repulsion to name the poles of an unlabelled magnet."]},"familyTip":"With two fridge magnets or bar magnets, let your child guess first: will these two ends push or pull? Then flip one magnet and try again. Ask them to explain the result using the words like and unlike poles.","keyIdea":"Every magnet has two poles, a north pole (N) and a south pole (S), where its pull is strongest. A freely hanging magnet turns until its north pole points north. When two magnets meet, like poles repel (N and N, or S and S, push apart) and unlike poles attract (N and S pull together).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Two magnets come close. Will these poles pull together or push apart? Drag each pair, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["Pull together (attract)","Push apart (repel)"],"items":[["N faces S",0],["S faces N",0],["N faces N",1],["S faces S",1],["Two north ends meet",1],["A south end meets a north end",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpoles","caption":"Flagship lab: swing a magnet to north, push and pull poles, fill a results table, test clips, name a mystery magnet's poles.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_-p_xp4hMvh":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which surface makes the most friction?","choices":["ice","glass","sandpaper"],"answer":2,"hint":"Think about which surface feels the roughest.","explain":"Sandpaper is rough and full of tiny bumps that catch, so it makes the most friction. Ice and glass are smooth."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A rolling ball slows down and stops on grass. Why?","choices":["Friction from the grass slows it down","The push it was given ran out","The grass pushes it forward"],"answer":0,"hint":"A force is needed to slow something down.","explain":"The ball does not stop because a push ran out. Friction between the ball and the grass acts against its motion and slows it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Why do people put grit or sand on icy paths?","choices":["to make more friction so feet grip","to make the ice more slippery","to make the path smoother"],"answer":0,"hint":"Grit makes the surface rougher.","explain":"Grit makes the surface rougher, so there is more friction and shoes can grip without slipping."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A block starts to slide on a wooden ramp at {a}°. On a rougher ramp it needs {b}° more. At what angle does it slide on the rough ramp?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the extra degrees to the wooden ramp angle.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}°. The rougher ramp has more friction, so it must be steeper before the block slides.","params":{"a":[15,25],"b":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"You push a box to the left. Which arrow shows friction on the box?","choices":["an arrow pointing right","an arrow pointing left","an arrow pointing down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Friction acts against the motion.","explain":"The box moves left, so friction points right. An arrow pointing down would show gravity, not friction."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which of these shows friction being useful?","choices":["bike brakes stopping a wheel","a sledge slowing on a path","a heavy box that is hard to drag"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the one where we want things to slow or grip.","explain":"Brakes work by rubbing pads on the wheel, making friction on purpose. The other two show friction getting in the way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"To test which mat makes the most friction, what must you change?","choices":["only the mat","the mat and the car","the mat and the push"],"answer":0,"hint":"A fair test changes one thing only.","explain":"Change only the mat and keep the car and push the same. Then any difference in distance must come from the mat."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"With the same push, a car rolls {a} cm on lino and twice as far on ice. How far does it roll on ice, in cm?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Twice as far means multiply by 2.","explain":"{a} × 2 = {=2*a} cm. Ice is smoother than lino, so there is less friction to slow the car.","params":{"a":[50,150]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A toy car gets the same push on carpet and on a smooth floor. Where does it roll further?","choices":["on the carpet","on the smooth floor","the same on both"],"answer":1,"hint":"Which surface rubs more against the wheels?","explain":"The smooth floor makes less friction, so the car keeps rolling for longer. Carpet is rougher, so it slows the car sooner."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"You slide a book to the right across a table. Which way does friction act on the book?","choices":["to the right","to the left","upwards"],"answer":1,"hint":"Friction always acts against the way the thing is moving.","explain":"The book moves right, so friction acts to the left. That is why the book slows down and stops."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A car rolls {a} cm on wood and {b} cm on carpet with the same push. How many cm further does it go on wood?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Take the carpet distance away from the wood distance.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} cm. It goes further on wood because wood makes less friction than carpet.","params":{"a":[120,180],"b":[40,90]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"In a fair test, a car rolls {a} cm on tiles. On sandpaper it rolls only a quarter as far. How far does it roll on sandpaper, in cm?","answer":"a/4","where":"a%4==0","hint":"A quarter means divide by 4.","explain":"{a} ÷ 4 = {=a/4} cm. Sandpaper is much rougher than tiles, so it makes much more friction.","params":{"a":[80,200]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Ava tests wood with a gentle push and carpet with a hard push. Why is this not a fair test?","choices":["She changed the push as well as the floor","She used two different floors","She used the same car"],"answer":0,"hint":"In a fair test you change only one thing.","explain":"Changing the floor is the point of the test. Changing the push as well means you cannot tell which change made the difference."}],"explain":{"kid":["When two surfaces rub, they make a force called friction. Friction slows moving things down. It always pulls the other way to the way a thing is moving.","Rough surfaces have more friction. Push a toy car on carpet and it soon stops. Give it the same push on ice and it rolls a long way, because smooth ice has less friction.","To compare two floors, do a fair test. Use the same car and the same push. Change only the floor. Then you know the floor made the difference.","Friction is useful too. It lets your shoes grip so you can walk, and it lets bike brakes stop the wheels. On ice there is too little friction, so you slip."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This matches Year 3 Forces and magnets in England: compare how things move on different surfaces. It builds on Pushes and Pulls and on Forces Vocabulary, and comes before contact and non-contact forces.","Friction is a contact force. It acts along the surfaces that touch, and it acts against the motion (or against the way a still object would start to slide). Rough surfaces have more bumps that catch, so they make more friction than smooth ones. The car distances and slide angles in the lab are a simple model, not measured data, but the order (ice, wood, carpet, sandpaper) matches what classes find.","Common mistakes: thinking a moving thing stops because the push runs out (friction stops it); drawing friction in the same direction as the motion; thinking soft carpet lets things roll further; thinking friction is always bad; and changing the push as well as the surface, which makes the test unfair.","In the lab your child rolls the same car on four floors, drags a block both ways and sees friction point backwards, runs a fair test with the same push, ranks floors from slippery to grippy, tilts a ramp until a block slides, and walks across ice with smooth and grippy soles."]},"familyTip":"Roll the same toy car down the same book ramp onto a rug, a wooden floor and a tiled floor. Keep the push the same each time and measure how far it rolls with a tape measure or in footsteps.","keyIdea":"Friction is a force between two surfaces that rub together. It always acts against the motion. Rough surfaces like carpet and sandpaper make more friction than smooth ones like ice, so a toy car given the same push stops sooner on a rough floor.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"More friction or less friction? Drag each surface into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["more friction (rough)","less friction (smooth)"],"items":[["sandpaper",0],["ice",1],["carpet",0],["polished floor",1],["grass",0],["glass",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfrict","caption":"Flagship lab: roll a car on four floors, see friction point backwards, run a fair test, tilt a ramp and walk on ice.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_lcf8lx-LkZ":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is friction a contact force?","choices":["it needs two surfaces rubbing together","you cannot see it","it only happens on ice"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the two things need to touch for friction to act?","explain":"Friction only acts where two surfaces touch and rub. Being invisible does not make a force non-contact."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"An apple falls from a tree. Which force pulls it to the ground?","choices":["friction","gravity","a push from the branch"],"answer":1,"hint":"Nothing is touching the apple as it falls.","explain":"Gravity is the Earth pulling the apple across the gap. It is a non-contact force, like magnetism."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A magnet holds a drawing on the fridge. What does this show?","choices":["magnets only pull paper","a magnetic force can act through thin paper","the paper is made of iron"],"answer":1,"hint":"What is the magnet really pulling on?","explain":"The magnet pulls on the steel fridge door through the thin paper. The paper just gets trapped in between."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two magnets with like poles facing are on a pencil. The top one hangs in the air. What holds it up?","choices":["nothing, it floats on air","the bottom magnet pushing it across the gap","the pencil gripping it"],"answer":1,"hint":"Like poles repel. What is the bottom magnet doing to the top one?","explain":"The bottom magnet repels the top one across the gap. That push is a non-contact force holding it up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which group lists only non-contact forces?","choices":["gravity and magnetism","friction and gravity","a kick and magnetism"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cross out any force that needs touching.","explain":"Gravity and magnetism both act across a gap. Friction and a kick need touching, so they are contact forces."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"You hold your hand 5 cm from a toy car and push the air. Why does the car not move?","choices":["your push is a contact force and you are not touching","the car is too heavy for any push","pushes only work on balls"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a push need to work?","explain":"A push from your hand is a contact force. With a gap, nothing passes from your hand to the car."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mo puts a thick book between a magnet and a clip, and the clip falls. Why?","choices":["the book makes the gap too big for the pull","books are magnetic","magnets never work through anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Paper and card did not stop the pull. What is different about a thick book?","explain":"The book pushes the magnet and clip far apart. Magnetic pull weakens with distance, so it is too weak to hold the clip."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"A magnet pulls a clip from {a} cm away. A second magnet pulls it from twice as far. How far away, in cm, does the second magnet pull the clip?","answer":"2*a","hint":"Twice as far means multiply by 2.","explain":"{a} × 2 = {=2*a} cm. The second magnet is stronger, so its non-contact pull reaches across a bigger gap.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"A clip jumps to a magnet when the gap is {b} cm or less. The clip starts {a} cm away. How many cm must the magnet move before the clip jumps?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the difference between the starting gap and the jumping gap.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} cm. The magnet never touches the clip: its pull acts across the last {b} cm.","params":{"a":[8,12],"b":[2,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A magnet pulls a paper clip towards it from 2 cm away. What kind of force is this?","choices":["a contact force","a non-contact force","not a force"],"answer":1,"hint":"Were the magnet and the clip touching when the clip started to move?","explain":"The clip moved while there was still a gap, so the magnetic force acted at a distance. That makes it a non-contact force."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which of these is a contact force?","choices":["gravity pulling a ball down","a magnet pulling a nail","your hand pushing a trolley"],"answer":2,"hint":"Look for the one where the two things must touch.","explain":"Your hand must touch the trolley to push it, so it is a contact force. Gravity and magnetism both act across a gap."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A paper clip is {a} cm from a magnet. The clip jumps when the gap is 3 cm or less. How many cm closer must you slide the magnet?","answer":"a-3","hint":"Take 3 away from the gap you start with.","explain":"{a} − 3 = {=a-3} cm. Once the gap is 3 cm, the pull is strong enough to move the clip without the magnet touching it.","params":{"a":[5,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam's magnet first pulls a clip from {a} cm away. Zoe's weaker magnet only pulls it from {b} cm away. How many cm bigger is the gap for Sam's magnet?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the difference between the two gaps.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} cm. A stronger magnet can pull across a bigger gap, but both pulls are non-contact forces.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"You slowly move a magnet away from a pin. What happens to its pull on the pin?","choices":["it gets weaker","it gets stronger","it stays the same until it suddenly stops"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the chart in the lab as the gap grew.","explain":"A magnet's pull fades bit by bit as the gap grows. It does not switch off at one fixed distance."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some forces need two things to touch. When you kick a ball, your foot must touch it. When you open a door, your hand pulls on the handle. These are contact forces. Friction is one too, because two surfaces rub.","Some forces work across a gap. Slide a magnet slowly towards a paper clip. The clip jumps to the magnet before they touch. A force that acts at a distance is a non-contact force.","The gap matters. A magnet pulls hard when it is close and more weakly as you move it away. A push from your hand stops as soon as there is any gap at all.","Gravity is a non-contact force too. Nothing touches a falling apple, but the Earth pulls it down. A magnet can even pull through thin paper or card, which is how notes stay on a fridge."],"adult":["Ages 7 to 8. This is the Year 3 Forces and magnets requirement in England: notice that some forces need contact between two objects, but magnetic forces can act at a distance. It follows Friction & Surfaces and Magnets Pull Some Things, and leads to work on gravity and air resistance in Year 5.","A contact force acts only where two objects touch: pushes, pulls by hand or rope, friction and air resistance. A non-contact force acts across a gap: magnetism, gravity and the electrostatic force. Non-contact forces weaken as the distance grows. The pull values in the lab chart are a simple model, not measurements, but the shape matches what a class finds with a ruler, a fridge magnet and a paper clip.","Common mistakes: thinking a magnet must touch to pull. Thinking its pull switches off at a fixed distance, when it fades. Calling friction non-contact because it cannot be seen. Believing any material blocks magnetism; thin paper and card do not. And thinking a floating magnet rests on nothing, when the other magnet pushes it up.","In the lab your child tries to push a ball from a gap, then while touching. They slide a magnet until a clip jumps across the gap. They chart hand push and magnet pull at gaps from 0 to 5 cm. Then they test the pull through paper, card and a thick book, sort six forces, and make a ring magnet float."]},"familyTip":"Lay a paper clip on the table and slide a fridge magnet towards it along a ruler. Note the gap when the clip first jumps. Then try again with a sheet of paper, then a thick book, between them.","keyIdea":"Some forces only work when two things touch: a kick, a push on a door, friction. These are contact forces. Other forces act across a gap: a magnet pulls a paper clip before it touches it, and gravity pulls a falling apple. These are non-contact forces, and they get weaker as the gap grows.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the force need touching? Drag each force into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["contact force (touching)","non-contact force (across a gap)"],"items":[["kicking a football",0],["a magnet pulling a pin",1],["friction on a sliding box",0],["a dropped cup falling",1],["pulling a rope",0],["two magnets pushing apart",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaggapforce","caption":"Flagship lab: push a ball with and without touching, pull a clip across a gap, measure pull against gap, test through card, sort forces and float a magnet.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_AvrQauS_zX":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does an apple fall when it comes off a tree?","choices":["gravity pulls it towards the Earth","the air pushes it down","heavy things want to go down"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which force acts between the Earth and every object?","explain":"Once nothing supports it, gravity, the pull between the Earth and the apple, makes it fall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A flat sheet of paper falls more slowly than a book. Why?","choices":["air resistance slows the wide, light sheet much more","gravity does not pull on paper","paper is too light to fall"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens if you scrunch the paper into a ball?","explain":"Air pushes up on the wide sheet. Scrunched into a ball, it falls almost as fast as the book. Gravity pulls on both."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A lamp hangs from the ceiling on a cord. Which forces act on it?","choices":["gravity down and the cord pulling up","only the cord pulling up","no forces, because it is not moving"],"answer":0,"hint":"Something must balance the pull of gravity.","explain":"Gravity pulls the lamp down and the cord pulls up just as hard, so the forces balance. Cut the cord and it falls."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Weight is measured in…","choices":["newtons, because weight is a force","kilograms, like mass","metres"],"answer":0,"hint":"Weight is the pull of gravity. What unit is used for forces?","explain":"Weight is a force, so it is measured in newtons with a forcemeter. Mass is measured in kilograms."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A dog has a mass of {a} kg. About how much does it weigh on Earth, in newtons?","answer":"10*a","hint":"About 10 N for every kilogram.","explain":"Each kilogram is pulled with about 10 N, so {a} kg × 10 N per kg = {=10*a} N. Its mass stays {a} kg; its weight is {=10*a} N.","params":{"a":[3,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Two bags hang on forcemeters. One reads {=10*a} N and the other reads {=10*b} N. About how many kilograms heavier is the first bag?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Turn each reading into kilograms by dividing by 10, then subtract.","explain":"{=10*a} N is about {a} kg and {=10*b} N is about {b} kg, so the first bag is about {=a-b} kg heavier.","params":{"a":[8,15],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"On the Moon there is no air. An astronaut drops a hammer and a feather together. What happens?","choices":["they land at the same time","the hammer lands first","the feather floats away"],"answer":0,"hint":"What slows a feather on Earth?","explain":"With no air resistance, gravity makes both speed up at the same rate, so they land together. Astronaut David Scott did this in 1971."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Rain falls on a hillside. Where does the water go?","choices":["downhill, pulled by gravity","uphill to the top","it stays exactly where it lands"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which way does gravity pull?","explain":"Gravity pulls the water down the slope, so it runs downhill into streams, lakes and the sea."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A book rests on a table. Which statement is true?","choices":["gravity pulls it down and the table pushes it up just as hard","no forces act on it because it is still","gravity switches off while it is on the table"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does gravity stop when something stops moving?","explain":"Gravity still pulls the book down. The table pushes up with the same size of force, so the forces balance and the book stays still."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A 4 kg ball and a 1 kg ball, the same size, are dropped from the same height at the same time. What happens?","choices":["they land at almost the same time","the 4 kg ball lands much sooner","the 1 kg ball lands first"],"answer":0,"hint":"Air resistance is about the same on two balls of the same size.","explain":"Gravity makes all objects speed up at the same rate. With the same size and shape, air slows them about the same, so they land together."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"On Earth, 1 kg weighs about 10 N. About how many newtons does a {a} kg bag weigh?","answer":"10*a","hint":"Multiply the mass in kilograms by 10.","explain":"{a} kg × 10 N per kg = {=10*a} N. More mass means a bigger pull from gravity.","params":{"a":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A forcemeter shows that a box weighs {=10*a} N on Earth. About what is its mass in kilograms?","answer":"a","hint":"Each kilogram weighs about 10 N, so divide by 10.","explain":"{=10*a} N ÷ 10 N per kg = {a} kg. Weight is the force in newtons; mass is the amount of stuff in kilograms.","params":{"a":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A girl in New Zealand, on the far side of the Earth from the UK, drops her pencil. Which way does it fall?","choices":["towards the ground at her feet, towards the Earth's centre","up, away from the Earth","sideways"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gravity pulls towards the centre of the Earth.","explain":"Down means towards the centre of the Earth wherever you stand, so her pencil falls to the ground just as it would in the UK."}],"explain":{"kid":["Gravity is a force. It is a pull between the Earth and every object. It pulls things towards the Earth, even when they are not moving. It is why you come back down when you jump, and why rain runs downhill.","A book on a table does not fall. Gravity pulls it down, but the table pushes it up just as hard. The two forces balance. Take the table away and the book is unsupported. Now only gravity acts, so the book falls. It gets faster as it falls.","Heavy things do not fall faster. Drop a big and a small ball of the same size together and they land at the same time. A feather is slow because air pushes up on it. With no air, a feather and a hammer land together. An astronaut showed this on the Moon in 1971.","The Earth is a ball, so gravity pulls towards its middle. That is why people in Australia do not fall off. Weight is the pull of gravity. We measure it in newtons with a forcemeter. One kilogram weighs about 10 newtons on Earth."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 11. This matches Year 5 Forces in the National Curriculum in England (explain that unsupported objects fall towards the Earth because of the force of gravity acting between the Earth and the falling object) and NGSS 5-PS2-1 (support an argument that the gravitational force exerted by Earth on objects is directed down). It builds on Contact & Non-Contact Forces, Forces Vocabulary and Drawing Force Diagrams, and leads into air resistance and mass versus weight.","Gravity is a non-contact force of attraction between any two masses; the Earth's pull on nearby objects is what we call weight. On Earth the gravitational field strength is about 9.8 N/kg, rounded to 10 N/kg at this age. A heavier object is pulled harder but has proportionally more mass to accelerate, so without air resistance every object falls with the same acceleration. 'Down' means towards the centre of the Earth.","Common mistakes: thinking gravity only acts on falling things, so a resting book has no forces on it. Believing heavier objects fall much faster, which air resistance on light, wide objects seems to confirm. Thinking air pushes things down. Imagining 'down' as the bottom of a map, so people on the far side of the globe would fall off. And mixing up mass, in kilograms, with weight, a force in newtons.","In the lab your child shows the balanced forces on a book, a lamp and a ball, then takes each support away and watches them fall. They race a heavy and a light ball, then a feather and a ball with and without air. Then they drop balls from people all round a globe and hang masses on a forcemeter."]},"familyTip":"Hold a book and a sheet of paper at the same height and let go: the book wins. Now scrunch the paper into a tight ball and try again. They land together, because only the air was slowing the flat sheet. Ask what is holding up each thing in the room.","keyIdea":"Gravity is a pulling force between the Earth and every object. It pulls things towards the centre of the Earth all the time. A book on a table does not fall because the table pushes up just as hard. Take the support away and the object is unsupported, so it falls. Heavy and light objects fall at the same rate; only air resistance slows wide, light things like feathers. Weight is the pull of gravity, measured in newtons: about 10 N for each kilogram.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is something holding it up? Drag each card into a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["supported: something holds it up","unsupported: gravity makes it fall"],"items":[["a book on a shelf",0],["an apple that has come off its branch",1],["a lamp hanging on a string",0],["a ball rolling off the edge of a table",1],["a picture on a hook",0],["a raindrop leaving a cloud",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdrop","caption":"Flagship lab: show the forces on held-up objects, take the supports away, race heavy against light, drop balls round the globe and weigh masses.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Q2k3fSwyzQ":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is it harder to lift someone on a see-saw if you sit close to the middle?","choices":["your effort arm is shorter, so you must push harder","the other person gets heavier as you move in","the pivot stops working near the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare how far you are from the pivot with how far the load is.","explain":"Effort × effort arm = load × load arm. A shorter effort arm means a larger effort. The load does not change; the pivot still works."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which sentence about a single flagpole pulley is right?","choices":["it changes the direction of the pull, not the weight of the flag","it always halves the force, like two pulleys would","it makes the flag lose mass as it goes over the wheel"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about what a single fixed wheel does to the rope.","explain":"A fixed pulley turns the rope over the wheel so you can pull down. The effort still matches the load (ignoring friction). Two supporting ropes are needed to share, and so to cut, the force."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A load of {a} sits 2 spaces from the pivot. You sit 4 spaces away. What push balances it?","answer":"a/2","hint":"You sit twice as far from the pivot as the load, so you push half as hard.","explain":"Effort × 4 = {a} × 2, so effort = {=a/2}.","params":{"a":[10,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A small gear has {s} teeth and a large gear has {=2*s} teeth. The small gear turns {n} times. How many times does the large gear turn?","answer":"n/2","hint":"The large gear has twice as many teeth, so it turns half as many times.","explain":"Turns × teeth match: {n} × {s} = (large turns) × {=2*s}. So the large gear turns {=n/2} times.","params":{"s":[8,12],"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"On a bicycle, a low gear makes a hill easier because…","choices":["you pedal more turns for each turn of the wheel","you pedal fewer turns and the bike leaps forward","the bike itself gets lighter on the hill"],"answer":0,"hint":"A low gear trades speed for an easier push, like a small gear driving a large one.","explain":"More turns of the pedals, each with a smaller force, give the same work as fewer hard pushes. The bike’s mass does not change."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Two pulleys share a load, so you pull with half the force. What is the catch?","choices":["you have to pull twice as much rope","the load loses half its mass","the extra pulley removes the need to do work"],"answer":0,"hint":"The smaller force has to act over a longer distance.","explain":"Two strands share the load, so you pull about half as hard and pull twice the rope. Work (force × distance) stays about the same."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A machine lets you push with a smaller force. What else must be true?","choices":["you push over a longer distance","you also push over a shorter distance and gain energy","the load itself becomes lighter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Machines change force and distance together. They do not make energy.","explain":"Work is force × distance. If the force falls, the distance you act over rises. The load does not lose mass, and you do not gain energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which example is a lever?","choices":["a crowbar turning on its contact point with the floor","a flagpole wheel that a rope runs over","two toothed cogs that turn each other"],"answer":0,"hint":"A lever is a bar that turns on a pivot.","explain":"A crowbar is a lever: the contact point is the pivot, the load is what you prize up, and your hands are the effort. The flagpole wheel is a pulley. Toothed cogs are gears."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"To lift a heavy friend on a see-saw with less push, you should sit…","choices":["farther from the middle (the pivot)","closer to the middle","exactly on the pivot"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how far your push is from the turning point, compared with the load.","explain":"A lever needs less effort when that effort is farther from the pivot than the load. Sitting closer, or on the pivot, makes you push harder or not turn the bar at all."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A single pulley on a flagpole lets you pull down to raise the flag. What else is true?","choices":["the size of the pull stays about the same as the flag’s weight","the flag becomes half as heavy","the pulley gives you extra energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"A single fixed pulley is there to change direction.","explain":"You pull down and the flag goes up: direction changes. Ignoring friction, you still pull about as hard as the flag weighs. Sharing the load between two ropes is what cuts the force."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A load of {a} sits 2 spaces from the pivot. You push 4 spaces from the pivot. What push balances the load?","answer":"a/2","hint":"Effort × 4 = {a} × 2. The products match, so the push is half the load.","explain":"Effort × 4 = {a} × 2, so the push is {=a*2/4} = {=a/2}. Sitting twice as far from the pivot as the load halves the push.","params":{"a":[8,16]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Two pulleys share a load of {w} equally on two ropes. Ignoring friction, what force do you pull with?","answer":"w/2","hint":"Each rope holds half of the load, so your pull matches one rope.","explain":"Two supporting ropes share {w}, so each holds {=w/2}. You pull with {=w/2}, but you pull twice as much rope.","params":{"w":[16,32]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A small gear with 8 teeth turns a large gear with 16 teeth. The large gear…","choices":["turns more slowly, with a stronger turning force","turns faster, with a weaker turning force","turns at the same speed and gives extra force for free"],"answer":0,"hint":"The large gear has twice as many teeth, so it turns half as far.","explain":"Two turns of the small gear make one turn of the large gear. The large gear turns more slowly and with a stronger turning force. You do not get extra force and extra speed together."}],"explain":{"kid":["A lever is a bar that turns on a pivot. The load is what you want to move. The effort is your push. If you push farther from the pivot than the load sits, you need a smaller force.","A see-saw is a lever. Sit farther from the middle to lift a heavier friend with less push. Sit close to the middle and you have to push much harder.","A pulley is a wheel with a rope. A flagpole pulley lets you pull down to lift the flag up. That changes the direction. One wheel does not make the flag lighter. Extra pulleys that share the load mean you pull with less force, but you pull more rope.","Gears are wheels with teeth that slot together. A small gear turning a big gear gives a stronger turn, but the big gear turns more slowly. A low gear on a bike makes a hill easier, but you pedal more times. A smaller force always acts over a longer distance."],"adult":["Ages 9 to 10. In the National Curriculum in England, Year 5 Forces asks children to recognise that some mechanisms, including levers, pulleys and gears, allow a smaller force to have a greater effect. The idea sits on moments (force × distance from the pivot) and on work (force × distance moved).","For a lever, effort × effort arm equals load × load arm. Moving the effort farther from the pivot reduces the effort. A single fixed pulley, as on a flagpole, changes direction: you pull down to lift up. Ignoring friction, the effort still matches the load. A movable pulley or block-and-tackle shares the load among the supporting ropes, so two strands mean about half the effort and twice the rope. Gears trade turns for turning force: teeth ratio 8 to 16 means the larger gear turns at half the rate, with about twice the turning force.","Common mistakes: sitting closer to the pivot to make a see-saw easier (that makes the effort larger). Thinking any pulley halves the force (a single fixed pulley does not). Believing a machine gives extra energy, so a smaller force can also act over a shorter distance. Mixing up bicycle gears: a low gear is easier up a hill because you pedal more turns.","In the lab your child drags a rider along a see-saw and watches the push fall as the effort arm grows. They pull down on a flagpole pulley and see the force stay about the same. They switch to two pulleys and see the pull halve while the rope doubles. They turn 8-tooth and 16-tooth gears both ways, then match force × distance on three machines."]},"familyTip":"On a see-saw, ask your child to sit close to the middle and then farther out: which way is it easier to lift you? Look at a flagpole: you pull down, the flag goes up. On a bicycle, try a low gear up a slope and notice that you pedal more times for an easier push.","keyIdea":"Levers, pulleys and gears can let a smaller force have a greater effect. On a lever, push farther from the pivot than the load sits. A single flagpole pulley changes the direction of your pull but not its size. Extra pulleys that share the load cut the pull, but you pull more rope. A small gear turning a large gear gives a stronger, slower turn. A smaller force always acts over a longer distance.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each thing a lever, a pulley or a gear? Drag each card, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["lever","pulley","gear"],"items":[["see-saw",0],["flagpole wheel and rope",1],["bicycle cogs",2],["crowbar",0],["well bucket over a wheel",1],["hand whisk cogs",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglever","caption":"Flagship lab: shift a see-saw rider, raise a flag with a fixed pulley, share a load on two ropes, turn gears, and see that a smaller force means more distance.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_cj0avUfUax":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A {m} kg car moves at {v} m/s. Find its momentum in kg m/s.","answer":"m*v","hint":"Multiply the mass in kg by the velocity in m/s.","explain":"p = {m} × {v} = {=m*v} kg m/s.","params":{"m":[800,1500],"v":[5,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"An object has momentum {p} kg m/s and mass 2 kg. What is its velocity in m/s?","answer":"p/2","hint":"Rearrange p = m × v to v = p ÷ m.","explain":"v = {p} ÷ 2 = {=p/2} m/s.","params":{"p":[2,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What is the unit of momentum?","choices":["kg m/s","J","N"],"answer":0,"hint":"Multiply the unit of mass by the unit of velocity.","explain":"kg × m/s = kg m/s. The joule is for energy and the newton is for force."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two 1 kg carts move apart, one at +3 m/s and one at −3 m/s. What is their total momentum?","choices":["0 kg m/s","6 kg m/s","−6 kg m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Momentum is a vector. Add the two values with their signs.","explain":"(+3) + (−3) = 0 kg m/s. Opposite directions cancel."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ali says a 60 kg runner at 7 m/s has 67 kg m/s of momentum. What did he do wrong?","choices":["He added mass and velocity","He forgot the minus sign","He used kinetic energy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at how 67 can be made from 60 and 7.","explain":"60 + 7 = 67. Momentum multiplies: 60 × 7 = 420 kg m/s."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A 0.16 kg cricket ball at 40 m/s and a 70 kg person walking at 1.5 m/s. Which has more momentum?","choices":["The person","The ball","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find m × v for each: 0.16 × 40 and 70 × 1.5.","explain":"Ball: 6.4 kg m/s. Person: 105 kg m/s. The slow but heavy person has more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why is momentum a vector but speed is not?","choices":["It uses velocity, which has a direction","It uses mass, which has a direction","Because it is measured in kg m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which part of p = m × v carries a direction?","explain":"Mass has no direction; velocity does. So p = m × v has the direction of the velocity."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A 2 kg cart at 3 m/s speeds up to 6 m/s. What is its new momentum?","choices":["12 kg m/s","24 kg m/s","6 kg m/s"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out p = m × v with the new velocity.","explain":"2 × 6 = 12 kg m/s, double the old 6 kg m/s."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A {m} kg trolley moves at {v} m/s. What is its momentum in kg m/s?","answer":"m*v","hint":"Use p = m × v with mass in kg and velocity in m/s.","explain":"p = {m} × {v} = {=m*v} kg m/s.","params":{"m":[2,40],"v":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which has more momentum: a 2000 kg truck at 5 m/s or a 70 kg cyclist at 10 m/s?","choices":["The truck","The cyclist","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out m × v for each one. Do not just compare the speeds.","explain":"Truck: 2000 × 5 = 10 000 kg m/s. Cyclist: 70 × 10 = 700 kg m/s. The truck has far more."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A {m} kg cart moves left at {v} m/s. Taking right as positive, what is its momentum in kg m/s?","answer":"-m*v","hint":"Moving left means the velocity is negative. Keep the sign in p = m × v.","explain":"v = −{v} m/s, so p = {m} × (−{v}) = {=-m*v} kg m/s.","params":{"m":[1,10],"v":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A ball of mass 500 g moves at 4 m/s. What is its momentum?","choices":["2 kg m/s","2000 kg m/s","4 J"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change grams to kilograms first: 500 g = 0.5 kg.","explain":"p = 0.5 × 4 = 2 kg m/s. Using 500 would make it 1000 times too big, and J is the unit of energy."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A cart's speed doubles while its mass stays the same. What happens to its momentum?","choices":["It doubles","It goes up 4 times","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"p = m × v: what happens to the product when one factor doubles?","explain":"p is proportional to v, so p doubles. Kinetic energy (½mv²) is the one that goes up 4 times."}],"explain":{"kid":["Momentum tells you how much motion something has. It depends on two things: how much mass it has and how fast it is moving. The rule is p = m × v.","Use mass in kilograms and velocity in metres per second. Then the unit of momentum is kg m/s. A 2 kg cart at 6 m/s has 2 × 6 = 12 kg m/s.","Mass matters as much as speed. A 2000 kg truck at 5 m/s has 10 000 kg m/s. A 70 kg cyclist at 10 m/s has only 700 kg m/s, even though the cyclist is faster.","Momentum has a direction. Pick right as positive. A cart moving left has a negative velocity, so its momentum is negative too. Two equal carts moving apart at the same speed have a total momentum of zero."],"adult":["Ages 14 to 16. GCSE physics (AQA 4.5.7.1, higher tier): momentum is defined by p = m v, with p in kg m/s, m in kg and v in m/s. NGSS HS-PS2-2 builds on this to show that total momentum is conserved when no outside force acts.","Momentum is a vector, so its sign shows direction. Because p is proportional to v, doubling the speed doubles the momentum. Kinetic energy, ½ m v², is the quantity that goes up four times. Momentum is the base for the next topics: conservation of momentum in collisions, and impulse, F Δt = Δp.","Common mistakes: adding mass and velocity instead of multiplying; using grams without converting (500 g gives a value 1000 times too big); dropping the minus sign for motion in the negative direction; mixing up momentum with kinetic energy or giving it in joules; and assuming the faster object always has more momentum.","In the lab you drag velocity arrows and watch signed momentum bars. You match a target value, slow a truck until it matches a cyclist, reverse a cart to make the total zero, double a speed, and convert grams before multiplying."]},"familyTip":"Compare a slow shopping trolley full of bottles with an empty one pushed at the same speed: which is harder to stop? Then ask which has more momentum: a 2000 kg van at 5 m/s or a 70 kg cyclist at 10 m/s, and work it out with p = m × v.","keyIdea":"Momentum is mass times velocity: p = m × v, measured in kg m/s. Because velocity has a direction, momentum is a vector: choose one direction as positive and anything moving the other way has negative momentum. A heavy, slow object can have more momentum than a light, fast one.","visual":{"type":"formula","caption":"Slide the mass and the velocity. The formula p = m × v gives the momentum in kg m/s.","opts":{"name":"p","expr":"m × v","vars":{"m":[1,20,2],"v":[1,10,6]},"unit":"kg m/s"}},"lab":{"type":"flagmom","caption":"Flagship lab: drag velocity arrows and watch signed momentum bars: match a target, slow a truck to a cyclist's momentum, reverse a cart, double a speed.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_3WMADSy0mA":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Both teams pull a rope with 7 N. What does the rope do?","choices":["Stays still","Moves towards the team that started first","Moves a bit, then stops"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equal pulls in opposite directions are balanced.","explain":"7 N each way are balanced, so the rope's motion does not change. It stays still."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A sledge is pulled forward with {a} N. Friction pulls back with {b} N. How many newtons more is the forward pull?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Find the difference between the forward pull and the friction.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} N more forward, so the forces are unbalanced and the sledge speeds up.","params":{"a":[6,10],"b":[1,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A book is resting on a table. Which is true?","choices":["Its weight and the table's push are balanced","No forces act on it","Only its weight acts on it"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gravity pulls the book down. Why does it not fall?","explain":"Weight pulls down and the table pushes up just as hard. The forces are balanced, so it stays still."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A car drives along a straight road at the same speed. What are the forces on it?","choices":["Balanced","Unbalanced, forward is bigger","Unbalanced, backward is bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the motion changing? Look at the speed and the direction.","explain":"The speed and direction stay the same, so the forces are balanced. A bigger forward force would speed it up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Team A pulls 9 N left. Team B pulls 5 N right. What happens?","choices":["The rope moves left","The rope moves right","The rope stays still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which side has more newtons?","explain":"9 N is 4 N more than 5 N, so the forces are unbalanced and the rope moves left, towards the bigger pull."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Left pulls a box with {a} N. How many newtons must the right pull be to balance it?","answer":"a","hint":"Balanced forces are the same size and go opposite ways.","explain":"The right pull must be {a} N too, the same size in the opposite direction.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia pulls a box with 2 N, then 4 N, then 6 N, and uses a heavier box each time. What is wrong?","choices":["She changed two things at once","She needed more tests","Nothing, it is a fair test"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many things change between her tests?","explain":"The pull and the box both change, so she cannot tell which one made the difference. Change only the pull."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A puck slides across ice and you push it from the side. What changes?","choices":["Its direction","Nothing, it is already moving","It stops at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"Unbalanced forces can change speed or direction.","explain":"A side push is an unbalanced force across the motion, so the puck turns."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"In a tug of war, the left team pulls with {a} N and the right team pulls with {b} N. How many newtons more does the left team pull?","answer":"a-b","hint":"The unbalanced part is the difference between the two pulls. Take the smaller from the bigger.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} N more to the left, so the forces are unbalanced and the rope moves left.","params":{"a":[7,10],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A box sits still on the floor. Its weight pulls down with {a} N. How many newtons does the floor push up?","answer":"a","hint":"The box is still, so the forces are balanced: equal in size and opposite in direction.","explain":"Balanced means equal, so the floor pushes up with {a} N.","params":{"a":[3,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A trolley rolls at a steady speed. The push is 4 N and friction is 4 N. What are the forces?","choices":["Balanced","Unbalanced, because it is moving","There are no forces"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the two forces. A steady speed means the motion is not changing.","explain":"4 N forward and 4 N back are equal and opposite, so they are balanced. The trolley keeps the same speed."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Leo tests how hard pulls move a toy car. Which test is fair?","choices":["Same car, same floor, only the pull changes","Bigger pull and a lighter car each time","A new floor and a new pull each time"],"answer":0,"hint":"A fair test changes only one thing.","explain":"Only the pull changes, so any difference in how the car moves must come from the pull."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A football rolls towards you and you push against it. What happens to it?","choices":["It slows down","It speeds up","It keeps the same speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"A push against the motion makes the forces unbalanced the other way.","explain":"Your push is against its motion, so the forces are unbalanced and the ball slows down."}],"explain":{"kid":["A force is a push or a pull. We measure it in newtons, or N for short. Two teams pull a box. If both pull with 6 N, the pulls are the same size but go opposite ways. They are balanced. The box stays still.","If one team pulls harder, the forces are unbalanced. The box moves towards the bigger pull and goes faster and faster.","Balanced does not mean stopped. A trolley that is rolling keeps the same speed when its push and friction are the same size.","To test fairly, change just one thing. Keep one pull the same and change the other. Then you can trust what you find."],"adult":["Ages 8 to 9. This is NGSS 3-PS2-1: plan and conduct an investigation to provide evidence of the effects of balanced and unbalanced forces on the motion of an object. In England, balanced and unbalanced forces are named at Key Stage 3, built on Year 3 and Year 5 work on pushes, pulls, friction and force meters. Key words: force, push, pull, newton (N), balanced, unbalanced, direction, motion, friction, fair test, evidence.","Balanced forces are equal in size and opposite in direction, so there is no change in motion. Unbalanced forces change the motion: the object starts, stops, speeds up, slows down or changes direction. The size of the change depends on the difference between the forces.","Common mistakes: thinking a still object has no forces on it (a book on a table has its weight down and the table pushing up); thinking a moving object must have an unbalanced force on it, when a steady speed means the forces are balanced; and changing two things at once in a test.","In the lab your child sets each pull in newtons, lets go and reads a trail of dots dropped every quarter second: even gaps mean steady speed, growing gaps mean speeding up. They run a fair test, keep a rolling trolley steady against friction and push a sliding puck three ways."]},"familyTip":"Play tug of war with a tea towel. Pull equally and it stays still; one of you pulls harder and it moves that way. Then roll a toy car across the floor and ask: what slows it down? Try it on a rug and on a smooth floor, keeping the push the same.","keyIdea":"Forces are pushes and pulls, measured in newtons (N). When two forces on an object are equal in size and opposite in direction, they are balanced, and the object's motion does not change: a still box stays still and a rolling trolley keeps the same speed. When one force is bigger, the forces are unbalanced and the motion changes: the object starts moving, speeds up, slows down or turns, in the direction of the bigger force. In a fair test you change only one force and keep everything else the same, so the results are evidence.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Balanced or unbalanced? Drag each to a box, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Balanced: motion stays the same","Unbalanced: motion changes"],"items":[["A book resting on a shelf",0],["A football you kick",1],["A tug of war where nobody moves",0],["A bike braking at a red light",1],["A car going along at a steady speed",0],["A swing starting to move when pushed",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagbalf","caption":"Flagship lab: set the pulls on a box, let go and read the dot trail, run a fair test, keep a rolling trolley steady, then push a sliding puck.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OUv-QXmW7_":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A walk: {a} km in 1 hour, a 30-minute stop, then {b} km in 1 hour. What is the average speed in km/h for the whole 2.5 hours?","answer":"(a+b)/2.5","hint":"Total distance ÷ total time.","explain":"({a} + {b}) ÷ 2.5 = {=(a+b)/2.5} km/h.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A line sloping down towards the time axis means…","choices":["returning towards the start","speeding up away","stopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"The distance from the start is shrinking.","explain":"Getting closer to the start."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A section rises {d} km in {t} minutes. What is the speed in km per minute?","answer":"d/t","hint":"Rise ÷ run.","explain":"{d} ÷ {t} = {=d/t} km per minute.","params":{"d":[1,9],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(d*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A curve that gets steeper and steeper shows…","choices":["speeding up","slowing down","stopped"],"answer":0,"hint":"Steeper means faster.","explain":"The speed is increasing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"At a steady {v} m/s for {t} s, how far do you go, in metres?","answer":"v*t","hint":"Distance = speed × time.","explain":"{v} × {t} = {=v*t} m.","params":{"v":[2,12],"t":[3,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How do you find the speed from a straight section?","choices":["Distance ÷ time for that section","Distance × time","Time ÷ distance"],"answer":0,"hint":"It's the gradient.","explain":"Speed = distance ÷ time."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The graph is flat from minute {a} to minute {b}. How long was the stop, in minutes?","answer":"b-a","hint":"End − start.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} minutes.","params":{"a":[2,15],"b":[16,30]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A flat line on a distance–time graph means…","choices":["stopped","steady speed","speeding up"],"answer":0,"hint":"The distance isn't changing.","explain":"No change in distance: not moving."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A cyclist rides {d} km in {t} hours at a steady speed. What is the speed in km/h?","answer":"d/t","hint":"Speed = distance ÷ time.","explain":"{d} ÷ {t} = {=d/t} km/h.","params":{"d":[6,60],"t":[2,5]},"where":"d%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A line rises from 0 to {d} m in {t} s. What is its gradient (the speed in m/s)?","answer":"d/t","hint":"Gradient = rise ÷ run.","explain":"{d} ÷ {t} = {=d/t} m/s.","params":{"d":[10,90],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(d*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which line shows the fastest movement?","choices":["The steepest one","The flattest one","The longest one"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gradient = speed.","explain":"The steepest line covers the most distance per unit of time."}],"explain":{"kid":["A distance–time graph shows how far you are from the start at each moment. Time goes across. Distance goes up.","A flat line means you have stopped. A line going up means you are moving away. A steeper line means faster. A line going down means you are coming back."],"adult":["The gradient of a distance–time graph is the speed: distance ÷ time for that section. A straight line means a steady speed.","Average speed for the whole trip = total distance ÷ total time, including stops. A curved line means the speed is changing; the gradient of the tangent gives the speed at that moment."]},"familyTip":"Sketch a graph of your walk to school: where did you stop, and where did you hurry?","keyIdea":"On a distance–time graph, flat means stopped, steeper means faster, and the gradient is the speed.","visual":{"type":"journey","caption":"Drag along the graph or press Play. Watch the walker: flat means stopped, steep means fast, down means going home.","opts":{"pts":[[0,0],[10,1],[15,1],[20,1.5],[30,0]],"tu":"min","du":"km","step":1}}},"mt_boNo2FHDNR":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A car accelerates steadily from rest to {v} m/s in {t} s. How far does it go, in metres?","answer":"v*t/2","hint":"Area of a triangle: ½ × base × height.","explain":"½ × {t} × {v} = {=v*t/2} m.","params":{"v":[4,20],"t":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A line sloping down towards the time axis shows…","choices":["slowing down","speeding up","going backwards at a steady speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Velocity is falling.","explain":"Falling velocity is deceleration."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A bus slows from {u} m/s to 0 in {t} s. What is the size of the deceleration, in m/s²?","answer":"u/t","hint":"Change in velocity ÷ time.","explain":"{u} ÷ {t} = {=u/t} m/s².","params":{"u":[6,30],"t":[2,6]},"where":"(u*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which graph shows constant acceleration?","choices":["A straight sloping line","A flat line","A curve getting flatter"],"answer":0,"hint":"The gradient stays the same.","explain":"A straight line has a constant gradient."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Speed {v} m/s for {a} s, then {v} m/s for {b} more seconds. What is the total distance, in metres?","answer":"v*(a+b)","hint":"One long rectangle.","explain":"{v} × {=a+b} = {=v*(a+b)} m.","params":{"v":[2,9],"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What are the units of acceleration?","choices":["m/s²","m/s","m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Velocity per second.","explain":"(m/s) per s = m/s²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Two cars: A's line is steeper than B's. Which has the greater acceleration?","choices":["A","B","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Steeper means more acceleration.","explain":"A greater gradient means a greater acceleration."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"A flat line on a velocity–time graph means…","choices":["moving at a steady speed","stopped","speeding up"],"answer":0,"hint":"The velocity is not changing.","explain":"Constant velocity: steady speed, not necessarily stopped."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Velocity rises from 0 to {v} m/s in {t} s. What is the acceleration in m/s²?","answer":"v/t","hint":"Gradient = change in velocity ÷ time.","explain":"{v} ÷ {t} = {=v/t} m/s².","params":{"v":[4,30],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(v*100)%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A runner moves at a steady {v} m/s for {t} s. What area is under the graph, in metres?","answer":"v*t","hint":"A rectangle: velocity × time.","explain":"{v} × {t} = {=v*t} m.","params":{"v":[2,9],"t":[5,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"What does the area under a velocity–time graph give?","choices":["Displacement","Acceleration","Speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Velocity × time = ?","explain":"Velocity × time is distance moved, so the area gives displacement."}],"explain":{"kid":["A velocity–time graph shows how fast something moves at each moment. Time goes across and velocity goes up.","A flat line means a steady speed. A line going up means speeding up. The steepness tells you the acceleration. The area under the line tells you how far it went."],"adult":["Gradient = change in velocity ÷ time taken = acceleration, in m/s². A negative gradient is a deceleration.","Area under the graph = displacement. Split it into rectangles and triangles: a car accelerating from rest to 10 m/s in 4 s travels ½ × 4 × 10 = 20 m."]},"familyTip":"On a car journey, note the speed every 10 seconds. Sketch the graph and find where the car sped up, slowed down and cruised.","keyIdea":"On a velocity–time graph, the gradient is the acceleration and the area underneath is the displacement.","visual":{"type":"area","caption":"Velocity v = 2t (speeding up from rest). Drag the end time: the shaded area is the distance travelled.","opts":{"mode":"velocity","f":"2*x","a":0,"b":3,"xr":[0,5],"yr":[-1,10],"ux":"m","tu":"s"}}},"mt_o2vTJv1KoM":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A bike slows from {u} m/s to 0 in {t} s. What is a, in m/s²?","answer":"-u/t","hint":"Final minus start.","explain":"(0 − {u}) ÷ {t} = {=-u/t} m/s².","params":{"u":[4,20],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(u*100)%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"a = {a} m/s² from rest for {t} s. What is the final velocity, in m/s?","answer":"a*t","hint":"v = u + at.","explain":"0 + {a} × {t} = {=a*t} m/s.","params":{"a":[2,6],"t":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"On a velocity–time graph, acceleration is…","choices":["the gradient","the area","the y-intercept"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rise over run.","explain":"Change in velocity ÷ time = gradient."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The unit m/s² means…","choices":["metres per second, per second","metres per square second of distance","metres times seconds"],"answer":0,"hint":"Velocity change per second.","explain":"(m/s) gained each second."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"a = {a} m/s², starting at {u} m/s, for {t} s. Final velocity in m/s?","answer":"u+a*t","hint":"v = u + at.","explain":"{u} + {a} × {t} = {=u+a*t} m/s.","params":{"a":[1,5],"u":[2,10],"t":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A car moves at a steady 30 m/s. Its acceleration is…","choices":["0","30 m/s²","1 m/s²"],"answer":0,"hint":"No change.","explain":"Velocity isn't changing, so a = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which has the greater acceleration?","choices":["0 to 10 m/s in 2 s","0 to 20 m/s in 10 s","They are equal"],"answer":0,"hint":"Work out both.","explain":"5 m/s² beats 2 m/s²."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"0 to {v} m/s in {t} s. What is the acceleration, in m/s²?","answer":"v/t","hint":"Change ÷ time.","explain":"{v} ÷ {t} = {=v/t} m/s².","params":{"v":[10,40],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(v*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What does 3 m/s² mean?","choices":["Velocity rises by 3 m/s every second","It moves 3 m each second","It takes 3 s to stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Per second, per second.","explain":"Each second, 3 m/s faster."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"From {u} m/s to {v} m/s in {t} s. What is a, in m/s²?","answer":"(v-u)/t","hint":"(v − u) ÷ t.","explain":"({v} − {u}) ÷ {t} = {=(v-u)/t} m/s².","params":{"u":[2,9],"v":[12,30],"t":[2,5]},"where":"((v-u)*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A negative acceleration means…","choices":["slowing down","going backwards","standing still"],"answer":0,"hint":"Velocity is falling.","explain":"Velocity drops: deceleration."}],"explain":{"kid":["Speeding up is called acceleration. A bike that goes 2 faster each second has an acceleration of 2.","Slowing down is a minus acceleration. We call it slowing down."],"adult":["a = (v − u) ÷ t, where u is the starting velocity and v is the final one. A car going from 0 to 20 m/s in 5 s has a = 4 m/s². Each second it gains 4 m/s.","If v is less than u, a is negative: the object is slowing down. On a velocity–time graph, acceleration is the gradient."]},"familyTip":"In a car or on a bike, notice the speed every few seconds while speeding up. Work out how much faster each second.","keyIdea":"Acceleration is how quickly velocity changes: a = change in velocity ÷ time, measured in m/s².","visual":{"type":"rate","caption":"Velocity v = 2t + 1. Drag the two points: the gradient between them is the acceleration.","opts":{"f":"2*x+1","a":1,"b":4,"xr":[-0.5,6],"yr":[-1,14],"ux":"s","uy":"m/s"}}},"mt_3AuVh--Vla":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"{d} m east in {t} s. What is the average velocity, in m/s east?","answer":"d/t","hint":"Displacement ÷ time.","explain":"{d} ÷ {t} = {=d/t} m/s.","params":{"d":[20,90],"t":[2,5]},"where":"(d*100)%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"{a} m east then {b} m west in {t} s. Average speed in m/s?","answer":"(a+b)/t","hint":"Distance ÷ time.","explain":"{=a+b} ÷ {t} = {=(a+b)/t} m/s.","params":{"a":[20,40],"b":[10,20],"t":[5,10]},"where":"((a+b)*100)%t==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"{a} m east then {b} m west in {t} s. Average velocity (east), in m/s?","answer":"(a-b)/t","hint":"Displacement ÷ time.","explain":"{=a-b} ÷ {t} = {=(a-b)/t} m/s.","params":{"a":[20,40],"b":[10,19],"t":[5,10]},"where":"((a-b)*100)%t==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A velocity of −3 m/s (east positive) means…","choices":["3 m/s west","slowing down","3 m/s east"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sign is direction.","explain":"Negative means the other way: west."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Can average velocity be 0 when speed is not?","choices":["Yes, if you return to the start","No, never","Only if you stop"],"answer":0,"hint":"Round trip.","explain":"Back to the start means zero displacement."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is a vector?","choices":["Displacement","Distance","Speed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Has a direction.","explain":"Displacement has size and direction."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Start at +{a} m, end at −{b} m. Displacement, in m?","answer":"-b-a","hint":"End − start.","explain":"−{b} − {a} = {=-b-a} m.","params":{"a":[2,9],"b":[2,9]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Walk {a} m east, then {b} m west. What is the displacement east, in m?","answer":"a-b","hint":"East +, west −.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} m east.","params":{"a":[20,60],"b":[5,19]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Walk {a} m east, then {b} m west. What is the distance, in m?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add every step.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} m.","params":{"a":[20,60],"b":[5,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"One full lap of a 400 m track. Displacement?","choices":["0 m","400 m","200 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do you end?","explain":"You end where you started, so 0 m."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Velocity is speed with…","choices":["a direction","a time","a mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Signed.","explain":"Velocity includes direction."}],"explain":{"kid":["Pick a direction to be positive, like east. Walking east is plus, walking west is minus.","Walk 30 m east, then 10 m west. You walked 40 m in all. But you are only 20 m east of the start. That 20 m is your displacement."],"adult":["Distance and speed ignore direction. Displacement and velocity are signed: choose a positive direction and give each move a sign.","Average speed = total distance ÷ time. Average velocity = displacement ÷ time. For 30 m east then 10 m west in 20 s, the average speed is 40 ÷ 20 = 2 m/s. The average velocity is 20 ÷ 20 = 1 m/s east."]},"familyTip":"Walk along a hallway, forward then back. Measure both the steps you took and how far you ended from the start.","keyIdea":"Displacement is how far you end up from the start in a chosen direction; average velocity is displacement ÷ time.","visual":{"type":"journey","caption":"Position east of the start: 30 m out, then 10 m back. Play it and compare distance with displacement.","opts":{"pts":[[0,0],[12,30],[15,30],[20,20]],"tu":"s","du":"m","step":1}}},"mt_hdlI0Sacpa":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Run one lap of a 400 m track. Displacement is…","choices":["0 m","400 m","200 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"End = start.","explain":"You end at the start: 0 m."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which pair is (scalar, vector)?","choices":["(speed, velocity)","(force, mass)","(velocity, distance)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Speed has no direction.","explain":"Speed is scalar, velocity is vector."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Acceleration is…","choices":["a vector","a scalar"],"answer":0,"hint":"Change in velocity.","explain":"It has a direction, so it is a vector."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"{a} m north then {b} m south. Displacement north, in m?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Opposite directions.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} m.","params":{"a":[20,50],"b":[1,19]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"{a} m north then {b} m south. Distance, in m?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Scalar: add.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} m.","params":{"a":[20,50],"b":[1,19]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A vector arrow's length shows…","choices":["its size","its direction","its time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Longer is bigger.","explain":"Length shows size; direction shows the way."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Energy is…","choices":["a scalar","a vector"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does it point anywhere?","explain":"Energy has no direction."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is a vector?","choices":["Velocity","Speed","Mass"],"answer":0,"hint":"Needs a direction.","explain":"Velocity has a direction."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Which is a scalar?","choices":["Temperature","Force","Displacement"],"answer":0,"hint":"No direction.","explain":"Temperature has no direction."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Two forces push right: {a} N and {b} N. What is the total, in N?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Same direction: add.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b} N.","params":{"a":[2,20],"b":[2,20]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"{a} N right and {b} N left. What is the resultant to the right, in N?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Opposite: subtract.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b} N right.","params":{"a":[12,30],"b":[1,11]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some amounts just have a size, like 5 kg or 20 seconds. These are scalars.","Others need a direction too, like 5 m north or a push to the left. These are vectors. We draw them as arrows."],"adult":["Scalars: distance, speed, mass, time, energy, temperature. Vectors: displacement, velocity, force, acceleration, momentum.","A vector arrow's length shows its size and its direction shows the way. Run one lap of a 400 m track: the distance is 400 m, but the displacement is 0, because you end at the start."]},"familyTip":"Name things around you and decide together: would a direction help describe it?","keyIdea":"A scalar has only a size; a vector has a size and a direction.","visual":{"type":"vectors","caption":"Drag the two arrow tips (or use arrow keys). Compare the distance walked (a scalar) with the displacement (a vector).","opts":{"a":[3,0],"b":[0,4]}}}});
